      "id": 1,
      "text": "Founded in 1837 and officially a city by 1881, Eaton Rapids earned the nickname “Saratoga of the West” thanks to its mineral‑rich waters.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.eatonrapidschamber.org/about-us/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Grand River Valley Railroad arrived in 1868, later becoming a Michigan Central branch, and helped launch the town’s industrial era.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://totallocal.com/eaton-rapids/history/",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1880, Samuel Horner purchased a woolen mill and expanded it into a successful factory complex that anchored local employment.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://totallocal.com/eaton-rapids/history/",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Visitors once flocked to Eaton Rapids bathhouses where mineral waters containing arsenic and radium were marketed as health cures.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eaton_Rapids%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
